From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] host: the prompt-bench step, so request preparation can be timed on this tree

Fork ai-engine, ref fork-ssh/ovn-up, commit ef81b4a49 "server: render and
tokenize a request once; TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE": the instrument the commit was
measured with. The measured work itself is patch 03, so this lands first and
gives the before number on our own tree.

What it is: `zig build prompt-bench` installs `zig-out/bin/prompt_bench`, run
as `prompt_bench MODEL PROMPTS [rounds]`. MODEL is a checkpoint directory with
tokenizer.json and a chat template; PROMPTS is a directory of the parity
probe's own texts (c0.txt, reply.txt, tool_plain.txt, tool_masked.txt, c32.txt,
fix40.txt). It builds four requests, two of them the probe's r2 and its masked
twin and two synthetic agent turns of about 200k and 650k tokens that end on a
tool result, and prints the median and minimum milliseconds a round for each
site of request preparation. No GPU, no network, no engine: the engine behind
the server is a script that accepts every submit and never replies.

It also checks itself each round: the prompt ids from chat.prepare, the ids
from prompt.prepare and the ids from a plain renderIds must be the same slice
of numbers, and the three marks (history_len, rewind_len, user_end) must agree
with the ones the request carries, or it stops with IdsDiffer or MarksDiffer.

No patch in this stack touches these files, so this completes nothing earlier.

What is not the fork's own lines, and why:
- zig/build/server.zig: the fork's promptBench builds the lanes, engine_api,
  tokenizer and template modules itself. Our tree has `plain`, which builds the
  same set plus `core` and runs addTo and testImports, and which every other
  CPU-only server artefact here is built with; our server root needs the core
  import, so promptBench calls `plain` and takes engine_api back out of its
  import table. The step name, its description and the executable are the
  fork's.
- zig/tests/server/prompt_bench.zig is the fork's file with the eight rows that
  time the new path removed: they call Rendered.keep and Rendered.pieces and
  messages.toolArgumentsWith, none of which exist before patch 03. What is left
  is the whole old path, which is the before number. Patch 03 puts the fork's
  eighteen rows back. The helper `render`, the local `problem` and the local
  `user_at` went with those rows for the same reason.
- two site labels lose a line number: the fork writes "systemPrefixLen (chat
  154)" and "replySpans (chat 177)" for lines in its own chat.zig, which are
  163 and 186 here and would drift again; they read "(chat.zig)" instead.
- zig/src/server/root.zig gains the fork's four exports. Three are what the
  bench imports; `messages` is used by the rows patch 03 restores.

What a GPU run has to say: nothing. prompt-bench is a host tool and is not part
of any default build step, the serve image or the host gates; it is only built
when asked for by name.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- build.zig grew a step line (cache_index_timing) under the hunk and root.zig's export block moved: the same added lines, seated by a 3-way merge; chat.prepare and plain() keep their signatures at v1.2.7.
- zig fmt: prompt_bench.zig's sites table is aligned for the rows it has here (the fold kept 0035's wider columns, which `zig fmt --check` refuses at this patch).
- source: work/fold/0004-prompt-bench.patch

